From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:31:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Atmosphere: the sky without a horizon

The physical sky is a planet's: a ray aimed below the horizon runs through
the planet as ground-level air, an even haze without clouds or stars, and a
moon there is a black disc. Maps that float in the sky or in space show it
over every edge.

rtx.atmosphere.noHorizon (bool, default False) shades a direction below the
horizon from one above it, per layer:
- the air's gradient from its mirror (noHorizonMirror): the sky-view LUT's
  bake marches the mirror's air into its lower half, continuous at the
  horizon, the twilight on the sun's side;
- the clouds and the high layer from its antipode (noHorizonCloudDir): the
  screen pass marches -dir, the high layer meets the antipode's shell, the
  secondary rays read the dome LUT's antipode texel. A mirror of the clouds
  read as a lake's reflection. A ray within 0.0015 of the horizon is held
  just above it, so the pass's |y| <= 0.001 skip leaves no bare line;
- every phase term from the true direction (evalAtmosphereRadiance's
  phaseDir, marchCloudLayers' phaseDirYUp, the high layer's phase, halos and
  arcs): the sun's aureole, silver linings and halos stay around the sun;
- the cloud bleed fades out over 6 degrees below the horizon: a dome texel
  carries the silver lining of its own direction;
- the stars and the night sky image without the horizon's fade, the
  airglow's boost from the mirror's elevation, the moons and their glow lit
  through the air of their mirror. The sun disc keeps the time of day.

The field takes the r18Pad0 slot (the buffer keeps its size), stays in the
sky-view LUT's key and is zeroed in the transmittance/multiscattering and
voxel-grid keys. Off, every site computes what it did before.

The dev menu: No Horizon under Atmosphere (the option's description as its
tooltip). Catalogs regenerated and translated; RtxOptions.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
